### Pi vs Radxa (headless server verdict: Radxa)

| Board | CPU | GB6 MT | Idle | Price |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ROCK 5C 8GB | RK3588S2 (8-core) | 2928–2962 | 1.6 W | $159 |
| ROCK 5B+ 8GB | RK3588 (8-core) | 2944 | 3.6 W | $175 |
| Pi 5 16GB | BCM2712 (4-core) | ~1400–1500 | 3.0 W | **$305** |
| Pi CM5 8GB + IO | BCM2712 (4-core) | ~1400–1500 | 2.5 W | ~$135 |

RK3588 Radxa ≈ 2× a Pi 5's multicore, lower idle, lower price. Pi's edge is software/accessory maturity — matters little for headless containers. **Caveat:** a used 1L x86 tiny PC (~$110–200) beats both on price *and* perf, esp. Jellyfin QuickSync — ARM's case here is watts/density/PoE, not cost.

### Scraper "wiring" (data-acquisition method decides Q6A load)

| Method | Board cost | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| Official API (JSON/REST) | ~0 | Mobilize, Bluesky, Mastodon, Reddit, ACLED, Eventbrite |
| RSS/Atom/iCal (conditional GETs) | ~0 | Indybay, local news, org calendars |
| Plain HTTP + parse (httpx+lxml) | low | static listing pages |
| Headless browser (Playwright) | **high**, leak-prone | last resort only: Instagram, X, Facebook Events |

Rule: **API/feed-first, browser-as-last-resort-and-isolated, poll on a 5–15 min schedule (not continuous).** Keep the churny DB local on the SSD; push only finished datasets/media to the NAS (SQLite/Postgres over NFS = locking/latency pain). Quarantine browser scrapers in a memory-capped container that recycles contexts; add zram. Wired this way the Q6A 8GB is near-idle.

