## How to read this document

Every figure is labelled. There are no unlabelled estimates — where nobody has published a number, the cell says **`no data`** rather than a plausible guess.

WARNING: **`no data` means "not found", not "does not exist".** The Radxa document's 2026-07-23 fact-check found eight cells marked `no data` whose values *were* published — several on pages it already cited for other figures. Treat a remaining `no data` as a prompt to look again, not as proof the measurement was never made.

| Tag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **M** | Measured by an independent third party |
| **V** | Vendor claim (datasheet, product brief, marketing) — not a measurement |
| **`no data`** | Nothing published. Do not extrapolate. |
| **`~`** | Estimate / unverified (the spreadsheet's own convention, carried through) |
| (AC) | Measured **at the wall** (AC plane — includes adapter loss) |
| (DC) | Measured at the **DC input** (inline meter — excludes adapter loss) |
| [X] | Product could not be verified to exist, or is cancelled |

**The AC/DC distinction matters and the two are not interchangeable.** Geerling and CNX measure at the wall; Bret Weber's `sbc.compare` measures at the DC input with a TC66C. A ~10–20% adapter loss sits between them. For rack/PDU budgeting you want the (AC) figures. No conversion factor has been applied anywhere in this document.

**Two provenance classes now coexist in the same tables.** Radxa rows carry third-party *measurements* with a named tester and a plane marker. Spreadsheet rows carry *vendor specs and street prices* verified by a spot-check pass — good data, but a different kind of data. A `~3 idle / ~10-12 load` figure from the spreadsheet is not comparable to a `1.6 (AC)` figure from `sbc.compare`. Where both exist for the same device, both are shown.

### Scope: what counts as a 10-inch rack node

Every device here physically fits a 10" rack (usable interior width ~222 mm between rails; 1U = 44.45 mm) **unless explicitly flagged `DOES NOT FIT`**. Non-fitting devices are kept as reference/warning rows rather than deleted — knowing *why* a popular box doesn't fit is worth a row.

WARNING: **Known fit warnings** (from the spreadsheet's own verification pass):

- **Do not fit between 10" rails:** GL.iNet Flint 2 (233 mm) · Cerebro Clusterboard (244 mm) · Minisforum MS-S1 Max · Milk-V Cluster 08 · Firefly Cluster Server R1 · QNAP QSW-M2106PR-2S2T · TRENDnet TL2-F7120 · Omada TL-SG2210MP · Omada TL-SG3210XHP-M2 · Zyxel GS1200-8HP v2 · MokerLink 8-Port 2.5G PoE.
- **Borderline — verify against your own rails:** Qotom Q20331G9 (221 mm) · Netgate 4200 (220 mm) · AOOSTAR WTR Pro (220 mm — orient the 185 mm side forward) · Mixtile Cluster Box (213 mm) · UGREEN DXP2800 (231 mm longest side — orient the 109 mm face forward).
- **Depth, not width:** Compute Blade blades are **250 mm deep** — your rack depth must be ≥260 mm.

**Rack Size (U) is descriptive, not arithmetic.** It reflects a real published mounting solution ("1U tray holds 2× Pi 5 side-by-side"), not a bare height fraction. `N/A – no published mount found` means you must design your own; a generic fallback is suggested in the row.

### Pricing and the 2025–26 RAM crisis

**Radxa pricing is from [arace.tech](https://arace.tech)**, pulled 2026-07-22/23 from per-variant Shopify data. **Everything else is July 2026 street/official pricing** from the spreadsheet's verification pass. MSRP = original launch price.

#### Which retailers can actually be price-checked — **Radxa only**

WARNING: **ARace and ALLNET China are Radxa-only retailers.** They carry Radxa's catalogue and nothing else — they are not a source for Raspberry Pi, Orange Pi, FriendlyElec, Minisforum, Jetson, Protectli, MikroTik, or any other brand in this document. For every non-Radxa row, pricing comes from that vendor's own store or street listings, noted per row in the pricing tables below.

Tested 2026-07-23. This matters because a retailer that can't be read is a retailer whose prices can only be repeated second-hand.

| Retailer | Scope | Machine-readable? | Use as |
|---|---|---|---|
| **[arace.tech](https://arace.tech)** | **Radxa only** | Yes — Shopify, per-variant price + stock exposed | **Primary for Radxa.** Most Radxa SKUs in stock; cheapest on most lines |
| **[shop.allnetchina.cn](https://shop.allnetchina.cn)** (ALLNET China) | **Radxa only** | Yes — Shopify, per-variant price + stock exposed | **Second Radxa source — verified working.** Radxa's long-standing distributor; stocks SKUs ARace has sold out, and vice versa. Always worth a second check |
| **AliExpress** | all brands | **No — blocked** | **Do not cite.** Item URLs bounce `www.aliexpress.com` → `login.aliexpress.com/sync_cookie_read` → `login.aliexpress.us/sync_cookie_write` → back to the item, an endless cookie-sync loop that never serves product data without a browser session. Any AliExpress figure in this document would be hearsay from a search snippet, not a read price — treat all such figures as **unverified** |
| Vendor direct (raspberrypi.com, friendlyelec, orangepi.net, Minisforum, Protectli…) | that brand only | WARNING: mostly readable | **The only source for the ~140 non-Radxa rows.** Neither ARace nor ALLNET stocks any of them |
| ameriDroid, Amazon, Evelta, distributors | mixed | WARNING: readable but frequently **stale on sold-out SKUs** | Cross-check only; never as the sole source for a live price |

Many current prices are badly inflated by the 2025–2026 LPDDR4/LPDDR5/DDR5 shortage: Raspberry Pi 5 16GB $120→**$305** (three hikes: Dec 2025 +$25, Feb 2026 +$60, Apr 2026 +$100), ZimaBoard 2 $159→$339, DGX Spark $3,999→$4,699, Orange Pi 5 Plus ~2.6× launch, Radxa X4 8GB $79.96→$265.99 (+233%). **Prices are volatile — re-verify before purchase.**

WARNING: The widely-circulated DRAM specifics (+90–95% QoQ in Q1 2026, a Raspberry Pi "seven-fold increase") appear in **none** of the sources used here and have been dropped rather than left uncited.


