## TL;DR for rackers

| If you want… | Pick | Caveat |
|---|---|---|
| **Lowest idle watts on a real NIC** | (AC) plane: **ROCK 5C** 1.6 W (**$69** 2 GB / **$159** 8 GB) (**Radxa CM5** 1.2 W as a module) · (DC) plane: **ROCK 4D** 1.5 W, then **E52C** 1.65 W | The two planes are **not** comparable — see "How to read". E52C and 4D sold out everywhere |
| **Best perf-per-watt** | **Radxa CM5** — 4.86 GFLOPS/W (AC) (module); **Dragon Q6A** 4.79 is the best standalone board (**$169**, 12 GB) | Only six boards have a comparable figure; see §2 |
| **Best all-round ARM rack node** | **ROCK 5T** (dual 2.5GbE, dual M.2, PoE) — **$179.90** (8 GB) | 12 V DC only, no USB-C power |
| **The benchmark x86 rack node** | **Minisforum MS-01** — 2× SFP+ 10GbE + 2× 2.5GbE, PCIe x16, 3× NVMe, vPro | 48 mm tall so it needs 1.5–2U; ~13–15 W idle; fan noise under load |
| **Cheapest capable node** | **Used 1L corporate tiny PC** (M720q / EliteDesk 800 G5 / OptiPlex 7080) | Used-only market; prices rising through mid-2026 |
| **NAS with real SATA** | ARM: **ROCK 5 ITX** (4× SATA on a Gen3 ×2 bridge, sold out) · x86: **Minisforum N5 Pro** or **AOOSTAR WTR Pro** | Set NAS expectations by the ITX's **~400 MB/s** 4-SSD RAID-0 figure, not the link rate |
| **Router / firewall** | ARM: **Radxa E52C** (dedicated PCIe lane per NIC, mainline OpenWrt) · x86: **Protectli VP2420** or **CWWK F2** · turnkey: **UniFi UCG-Fiber** | E52C sold out and has no M.2 |
| **Fastest ARM CPU** | **Dragon Q8B** — GB6 1683 ST / 7058 MT | 50 W Linpack transients cooked the PMICs |
| **Most ARM expansion** | **Orion O6** — PCIe x16 + dual 5GbE | 14.2 W idle, unfixed; x16 **cannot bifurcate** |
| **x86 for QuickSync / amd64 images** | **Radxa X4** (N100) in an SBC slot; **Beelink EQ14 / ODROID-H4+** for more headroom | X4 is 9.1 W idle (AC) — 3–6× an ARM node |
| **Dense low-power cluster** | **Radxa CM5 on DeskPi Super6C** (Radxa-certified, shipping) or **Turing Pi 2.5** if you want a real BMC | Super6C has no BMC. The denser Xerxes Pi is [X] **unverified** — see §13 |
| **Local LLM / vision AI** | **Jetson Orin Nano Super** ($249, 67 TOPS) — or **DGX Spark** if budget is not the constraint | 8 GB caps model size on the Orin Nano |
| **Out-of-band management** | **JetKVM** per node (cheap) or **PiKVM V4 Mini** (reference-class) | There is **no BMC anywhere in the Radxa lineup** — see §9 |
| **The power layer** | **Mean Well HDR-150-48** on DIN rail for 48 V PoE; UPS HATs per node | UPS HATs add real stack height — plan 2U |

**Six caveats that shape every decision below:**

1. **RK3588S / RK3588S2 / RK3582 have zero PCIe 3.0 lanes.** Any "M.2 NVMe" on ROCK 5A/5C/5C Lite/CM5 is **PCIe 2.1 ×1 (~450 MB/s)**. This is the single most consequential spec-sheet illusion in the Radxa lineup — see §4.
2. **There is no BMC, IPMI, or iKVM anywhere in the Radxa lineup**, nor on any shipping carrier that supports a Radxa module. The mini-rack world solves this with **Turing Pi 2.5** (real BMC), **Compute Blade** (PoE port cycling), or an external **PiKVM/JetKVM/NanoKVM** per node. See §9.
3. **PoE is only on SBC-class boards** and always via an add-on HAT. The Orions, Dragon Q8B and the entire Radxa E-series have **no PoE at all**. Most x86 mini PCs and NAS boxes are DC-barrel or internal-AC and cannot be PoE-fed at all.
4. **Almost nothing Radxa is in stock.** Seven Radxa board SKUs plus three accessories are buyable at ARace today. The x86/mini-PC/switch side of this document is far better supplied.
5. **Width is not the only fit constraint.** Height (48 mm needs 2U), depth (Compute Blade needs 260 mm), and stack height once a HAT or UPS is fitted all bite. See §11.
6. **The RAM crisis has reordered value.** Several ARM SBCs now cost more than a used 1L x86 tiny PC that outperforms them. Check §"Availability & pricing" before assuming an SBC is the cheap option.


