**Three rules the power layer imposes on a 10" rack:**

1. **UPS HATs add stack height, not just cost.** Every 21700/18650 HAT here needs ~2U once cells are fitted, and the spreadsheet notes that `mini-rack.jeffgeerling.com` itself only observes that UPS HATs are "tricky" to rack-mount without offering a design. Per-node battery is the *least* space-efficient way to get ride-through.
2. **A single 48 V DC plant beats N per-node UPS HATs** on cost, height, and number of things that can fail — the Mean Well HDR-150-48 on DIN rail is the reference part. It also feeds PoE directly, which is how you get ride-through on a PoE-fed shelf without touching the nodes.
3. **Cell prices are inflated too.** 4× 21700 adds $25–40 on top of a $40–50 HAT, so a "cheap" UPS HAT is a $75–90 accessory per node.

