### Density and the failure-domain tradeoff

**12× Radxa CM5 in 1U** (per-node figures from Geerling's CM5-on-IO-board measurements; totals are arithmetic). [X] **The 12-per-1U carrier assumed here is the unverified Xerxes Pi** — read the per-node column as solid and the ×12 column as a hypothetical:

| | Per node | ×12 |
|---|---|---|
| Cores | 4×A76 + 4×A55 | **96 cores** (48 big + 48 little) |
| RAM (32 GB SKU) | 32 GB | **384 GB** |
| NPU | 6 TOPS | 72 TOPS |
| HPL | 48.6 GFLOPS | ~583 GFLOPS |
| Idle | 1.2 W | **~14.4 W** |
| Load | 8.4 W | ~101 W |

Cost, **at the unverified Xerxes prices**: ~$198/node (PoE variant $79 + CM5 8/64 $119), ~**$2,376** per 1U plus a switch. **Density is not the constraint here — PoE budget and switch port count are.**

**The architectural choice:**

- **Shared-carrier** (Turing Pi 2.5 = 4, Super6C = 6, Super4C = 4, NanoCluster, Lichee Cluster 4A, Pine64 Clusterboard = 7): one carrier failure kills **every node on it** — a Super6C fault takes out 6 nodes at once, so a 3-node etcd quorum on one carrier is a *total* quorum loss, not a survivable minority failure. One PSU and one switch die feed all nodes. **Upside: these are the only designs with a BMC** (Turing Pi 2.5 has a real one; Super4C has ESP32 power control only). The Super4C is the only one that mitigates the power path, with dual 19 V inputs auto-switching and two independent NICs per node.
- **Blades** (Compute Blade, 20 per 1U): failure domain is **one node**. Uptime Industries states this as an explicit design goal — the shared component becomes a commodity PoE switch you can keep a cold spare of. Cost: no BMC; out-of-band degrades to "toggle the PoE port and let it netboot." **And remember the 250 mm depth.**

WARNING: **No confirmed shipping product gives you both a real BMC and validated Radxa CM5 support.** Turing Pi 2.5 has the BMC but its USB routing does not match the CM5; Super6C is Radxa-certified but has no management at all; the Xerxes Pi is claimed to have CM5 support and no BMC, but [X] could not be verified to exist.

WARNING: **The honest economic note the spreadsheet makes about the whole tier:** *"Buy for cluster-management experience, not price/perf — N150 mini PCs beat it per node on raw value."* A populated Turing Pi 2.5 runs $1,300–2,000. Four used 1L tiny PCs cost a fraction of that and are faster. Cluster boards win on **watts, density, and learning**, not on price/performance.

