## 14. Software / OS support

### Radxa platform maturity

| Platform | Maturity |
|---|---|
| **RK3588 / RK3588S / S2** | **Best.** Years of Armbian/Debian/Ubuntu, good mainline progress, huge community, mature vendor kernel. Safest for unattended uptime. |
| **RK3582 (E52C, 5C Lite)** | Same Rockchip base; **E52C has mainline OpenWrt (25.12+) and mainline Armbian 6.18**. E54C has **neither** — vendor kernel only. |
| **RK3576 (ROCK 4D, Radxa CM4)** | Newer. Debian 12 + Yocto/Buildroot/**Android 14** (newest Android in the lineup). |
| **Qualcomm QCS6490 (Q6A)** | Ubuntu 24.04 with a `-qcom` kernel works well; upstream Qualcomm Linux effort is real but young. |
| **Snapdragon 8cx Gen 3 (Q8B)** | **Standard UEFI + unified ARM ISO boot** — a genuine advantage. Benefits from Windows-on-ARM and 8cx Linux work, but only one independent test dataset exists. |
| **CIX P1 (Orion)** | Newest. Debian 12 / Ubuntu 25.04, active development, **firmware measurably changes performance**; peripheral maturity still catching up. |
| **Intel N100 (X4)** | **Best of all in one sense** — conventional UEFI, stock distro ISOs, Windows, PXE, no device trees or vendor kernels. Mainline `igc` NIC driver. |
| **Allwinner A527 (Cubie A5E)** | WARNING: **Weakest.** Mainline supports "basic headless use" only — **USB 3.0 and the second Ethernet port are still WIP upstream.** Full function requires Radxa's BSP kernel with closed binaries. The dual-NIC feature you'd buy it for is the part mainline doesn't drive. |

**For a set-and-forget rack, RK3588-family boards remain the low-risk ARM choice.** The Qualcomm and CIX boards trade some maturity for substantially more compute.

### The software argument for x86 in an ARM rack

**QuickSync**, concretely: on the N100, H.264 and HEVC (8- and 10-bit) have **hardware decode *and* encode**, and AV1 has hardware decode (AV1 *encode* is explicitly **not** supported on N-series — that needs Arc/DG2). Requires kernel ≥6.2 and Intel compute-runtime 23.xx+. A container gotcha: **if the container user isn't in the `render` group, transcodes silently fall back to CPU.** No ARM SBC here has an equivalent; a Pi 5 has no hardware HEVC encode at all.

Secondary reasons: **amd64-only container images** (still a real constraint for some self-hosted software), **AES-NI** (1230 Mbps OpenSSL on the X4), and **standard UEFI/PXE/WoL/BIOS-restore-on-AC-loss** — see §9.

**Counter-argument, stated fairly:** the x86 tier costs 3–6× the idle watts of the ARM tier for the same service load. A Radxa X4 idles at 9.1 W (AC) against a ROCK 5C's 1.6 W (AC); a Minisforum MS-01 idles at ~13–15 W. Over a year at $0.15/kWh that is the difference between $2 and $20 per node. **Run x86 where you need what only x86 gives you, and ARM everywhere else** — that is the mixed-rack recommendation in §15.

### OS support across the roster

Practical groupings rather than a 170-row restatement (full per-device strings are in the table below):

- **Proxmox** is the de-facto x86 rack hypervisor here and is explicitly called out on the MS-01, MS-A2, MS-A1, NUC 14 Pro, the 1L used boxes, and most NAS-oriented x86 nodes.
- **TrueNAS / Unraid / OMV** for the NAS tier; **CasaOS/ZimaOS** ships on the IceWhale boards.
- **OpenWrt / OPNsense / pfSense / RouterOS / VyOS** define the appliance tier — and the spreadsheet's own inclusion rule was that a router/gateway only qualifies if it is software-modifiable *or* is a Ubiquiti UniFi product.
- **Armbian** is the ARM SBC lingua franca; check the specific board page, not the SoC.
- **k3s / Talos** for the cluster tier; **JetPack / L4T** for Jetson; **Raspberry Pi OS / DietPi** for the Pi family.
- **macOS** exists in exactly one row (Mac mini M4) and is the only way to legally run it in a rack.


