## 15. Recommendations by rack role

### By rack role

- **Dense low-power services (DNS, monitoring, reverse proxy), PoE-fed:** **Radxa ROCK 5C** (1.6 W idle (AC), Pi footprint, 802.3at) — **$69.00** 2 GB / **$159.00** 8 GB. Two things weaken this pick: it's on **no documented PoE HAT compatibility list**, and the **Radxa 25W PoE HAT is itself out of stock at ARace** ($19.99). **ZERO 3E** (**$39.99**, 2 GB) if 1–2 GB is enough and you feed PoE from a **switch, never an injector** (its own $9.99 PoE HAT *is* in stock) — accept SD-only boot with no fallback. **Raspberry Pi 5** remains the pick if ecosystem and mount availability matter more than watts or price.
- **Router / firewall:** **Radxa E52C** on ARM — dedicated PCIe lane per NIC, 1.65 W idle, mainline OpenWrt, built-in console; ask for BOM rev v1.20A. Avoid E24C/E54C (~1 Gbps aggregate ceiling, no mainline OpenWrt). On x86: **Protectli VP2420** (fanless, warranty) or **CWWK F2** (ports per dollar). Turnkey: **UniFi UCG-Fiber**. RouterOS: **MikroTik RB5009UG+S+IN**.
- **NAS:** ARM — **ROCK 5 ITX** is the only real Radxa option (4× SATA on a Gen3 ×2 ASM1164, dual independent 2.5GbE, SMB Multichannel measured ~600 MB/s). Set expectations by the **~400 MB/s 4-SSD RAID-0** figure, not the 1.97 GB/s link. Budget 27.6 W idle populated plus an **unmeasured but non-zero soft-off draw**. Cheaper ARM route: a Radxa CM5 on a **Radxa Taco** ($65, in stock) for 5× SATA, or a **Penta SATA HAT on a Pi 5**. x86 — **Minisforum N5 Pro** (5-bay flagship) or **AOOSTAR WTR Pro** (4× 3.5", 220 mm so check rails). All-flash — **Beelink ME mini** or **GMKtec NucBox G9**.
- **Edge-AI / camera ingest with PoE:** **Radxa Dragon Q6A** — best perf/watt of any standalone board here (4.79 GFLOPS/W (AC)), 3× CSI, officially supported PoE HAT, and one of the few Radxa boards in stock at **$169.00 for 12 GB** — the best RAM-per-dollar of any in-stock Radxa SKU. For CUDA specifically: **Jetson Orin Nano Super** ($249, 67 TOPS) is the better tool and has a published 1U 10" mount.
- **Heavy compute / build node:** ARM — **Dragon Q8B** (fastest ST and MT; standard UEFI) or **Orion O6** (best memory bandwidth, PCIe x16; budget 14.2 W idle permanently). x86 — **Minisforum MS-01** if you want NICs and slots, **MS-A2 / MS-A1** if you want socketed AM5 CPU headroom, **Beelink SER9 / GEEKOM A9 Max** if you want single-thread speed in 1L.
- **Local LLM:** **NVIDIA DGX Spark** if the budget exists; **Jetson Orin NX 16GB** for a 1U-friendly middle; **Ryzen AI Max+ boxes (MS-S1 Max, GTR9 Pro)** for large unified memory — but MS-S1 Max **does not fit** a 10" rack.
- **Media transcoding / amd64 workloads:** **Radxa X4** in an SBC slot, or any N100/N150 mini PC for real headroom. Accept ~9–15 W idle, and on the X4 specifically: mandatory active cooling, an RP2040-mediated GPIO header, and marginal PoE.
- **Cluster:** **Radxa CM5 modules** on the densest carrier you can actually buy — **DeskPi Super6C** (6 nodes, Radxa-certified, shipping) is the safe pick at the cost of a 6-node shared failure domain. **Turing Pi 2.5** if you want a real BMC and will accept CM4/RK1/Jetson nodes instead. **Compute Blade** for one-node failure domains at 20/1U — check 260 mm rack depth. [X] The 12-node Xerxes Pi could not be verified to exist.
- **Out-of-band management:** **JetKVM** per node is the cost-effective answer; **PiKVM V4 Mini** is the reference-class one; **Sipeed NanoKVM** is the cheapest. All three work across every node type here.
- **The power layer:** **Mean Well HDR-150-48** on DIN rail for a 48 V PoE plant; **Waveshare UPS HAT (E)** or **Geekworm X1206** for per-node Pi ride-through — but plan **2U** once cells are fitted.

### The practical mixed rack

Several **ROCK 5C / CM5** nodes for services, one **E52C** as the router, one **ROCK 5 ITX** or an x86 NAS box for storage, one **N100/N150 x86 node** for transcoding and amd64 images, a **JetKVM** on anything you can't reach, and a **PoE switch sized by total budget, not port count**. Add an **Orion O6** or an **MS-01** only if you specifically need PCIe expansion or 10GbE.

**And the honest caveat over all of it:** under 2026 pricing, a used 1L corporate tiny PC frequently beats a new ARM SBC on both price and performance. The ARM case is watts, density, GPIO and PoE — not cost.


