## Known gaps — what nobody has published

Listed so they aren't mistaken for oversights. **Do not fill these by extrapolation from SoC class or PSU rating.**

### Gaps inherited from the Radxa document

**Power:** ROCK 5B+ idle (the single most consequential blank) · ZERO 3E idle · ROCK 5 ITX+ everything · E20C/E24C/E25/E54C everything · ROCK 5C Lite load · CM3/CM3I/CM3J/CM3S/Radxa CM4 all power · **ROCK 5 ITX soft-off draw** (the always-live ATX rail is documented; its wattage is not) · E52C DMC-governor power delta · the NVMe adder on every board · **PoE HAT conversion efficiency on all five HATs** (which makes every 802.3af verdict in §7 optimistic by an unquantified margin) · **no measurement of any Radxa board running through any Radxa PoE HAT exists**

**Benchmarks:** E54C/E25/E24C/E20C — no benchmark of any kind · Dragon Q5E, X2L, CM3J, CM3S, CM3, CM3I — nothing from any tester · ROCK 5 ITX+ everything · ROCK 5C Lite Geekbench · NX5 7-Zip single-thread · Q8B has only one independent tester · NPU inference for RK3582, T527, RK3528A and the Q8B's Hexagon · sysbench and STREAM essentially everywhere

**Topology:** CIX P1 total lane count · SC8280XP lane count · QPS615 upstream width on the Q8B · X4 lane allocation beyond its M.2 · ROCK 5T schematic-level PHY mapping · ROCK 5C/5C Lite combo-PHY assignment · Q6A onboard WiFi attachment

**Management:** auto-power-on on every Radxa board except the ROCK 5 ITX · hardware watchdog enablement on every board · SPI NOR presence on ROCK 5T and ROCK 5 ITX+

**Throughput:** E52C above 1 Gbps (every tester is ISP-limited) · E24C/E54C anything · routed NAT on E20C · **any >1 Gbps figure for Orion O6/O6N** · **NVMe** on Orion O6/O6N, Q6A, ROCK 5T, ROCK 5C, CM5, ROCK 4D

**Unresolved conflicts:** RK3576 process node (22 nm vs 8 nm; Rockchip states neither) · ROCK 5B idle (1.5/1.7/3.6/4.5 W) · ROCK 5B NVMe read (fio 3.09 GB/s vs iozone 1.04 GB/s vs sbc.compare 1.4 GB/s — same board, same class of drive) · Cubie A5E Geekbench (2.1× spread) · O6N idle (9.3 W measured vs ~15 W vendor) · ROCK 5 ITX PSU sizing with 4 HDDs (65 W docs vs 90 W brief — **use 90 W**) · ROCK 5 ITX RTL8125BG RX (2.32 vs 1.3–1.6 Gbps) · numerous E-series RAM/eMMC/dimension conflicts between Radxa's own briefs and docs

### Gaps created by the merge — what the spreadsheet does not carry

These are **not** flaws in the spreadsheet; they are columns it never had. They matter because the Radxa half of this document sets a standard of evidence the other 148 devices cannot currently be held to.

- **No independent benchmark of any kind for 148 of 170 devices.** No Geekbench, no `sbc-bench`, no HPL, no iperf3. CPU model is a reasonable proxy for the x86 tier and is **not** a proxy for the ARM cluster boards, the AI accelerators, or the switches.
- **No plane-marked power measurement for 148 of 170 devices.** The `~N idle / ~N load` figures are vendor or estimate class with the measurement plane unstated. **Do not put them in the same ranking as a (AC) or (DC) figure.**
- **No schematic-level PCIe tracing outside Radxa.** Every "PCIe x16 slot (x8 electrical)", "4× M.2 NVMe" and "2× 10GbE" claim in the wider roster is the vendor's own. §4 exists precisely because those claims mislead; nobody has done the tracing for these boards.
- **No measured storage throughput outside Radxa.** No fio, no iozone.
- **No measured PoE behaviour anywhere** — not the Radxa HATs (efficiency unpublished), and not the switches (per-port derating under full budget unmeasured).
- **No thermal measurement outside Radxa.** The thermal notes in §10 for the wider roster are reported complaints, not instrumented runs.
- **No acoustic measurement anywhere**, despite fan noise being a top-three complaint on the MS-01 and every Ryzen AI Max+ box, and despite it being the single most common reason a mini-rack ends up in a different room.
- **No verified auto-power-on / WoL / BIOS-restore behaviour** on the non-x86 devices — and on the x86 devices it is a documented BIOS *option*, which is not the same as a tested one.
- **Two devices carry explicitly Low confidence** in the spreadsheet's own verification pass: **Milk-V Cluster 08** and **Orange Pi X**, both pre-release.
- **Prices are the fastest-decaying field here.** The spreadsheet's own guidance is to re-verify every Current Price and any row whose Last Verified date is stale; those dates are preserved per-device in Appendix B.

### Claims withdrawn by the Radxa document's 2026-07-23 fact-check

Previously stated as sourced, now known to be unsupported: the ROCK 5 ITX "8 W in soft-off" figure and the "Radxa claims 1.5 W standby" counterclaim · a merged perf-per-watt ranking mixing two methodologies · "96 A76 cores" for 12× ROCK 5C · a spliced pseudo-quotation of Radxa's CM5/CM3-IO-board warning · three CM5-compatibility items absent from the FAQ · the Xerxes Pi's existence · Cerebro Kickstarter funding totals · DRAM percentage moves · "ARace is materially cheaper" · Dragon Q5E WiFi 7.

**Sources rejected as unreliable during research:** AI-generated content farms circulating fabricated ZERO 3E idle figures (0.8 W, 2.5 W) and a fake "940 Mbps/1.82 Gbit" throughput number · sbcwiki's ROCK 4D page (contaminated with another board's specs) · Boiling Steam's O6N review (republishes Geerling's **O6** power data under an O6N heading) · the "326.1/1693.7" Q6A "Geekbench" figure (it's CPU-Z under Windows). One widely-cited error corrected: Thomas Kaiser's ROCK 5 ITX article names the boot SPI NOR as a Winbond W25X20CL — per the schematic that part is the **ASM1164's** firmware flash; the actual boot flash is a **16 MB XT25F128BW**.


